WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.670
<v ->Okay, so next let's get into CloudWatch.</v>

2
00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:05.850
So CloudWatch has been everywhere through our discussions,

3
00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:07.470
but now it's time we take a deeper look

4
00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:09.090
at CloudWatch in specifics.

5
00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:11.520
So the first part is CloudWatch Metrics.

6
00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.070
And we already know that all the AWS services

7
00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:17.400
many of them provide you CloudWatch Metrics out of the box.

8
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.070
And for EC2 it's particular by default.

9
00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:21.900
The resolution is five minutes

10
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.150
for EC2 standard monitoring.

11
00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:26.790
But you can enable detailed monitoring for EC2

12
00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:29.970
which gives you EC2 metrics every one minute.

13
00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:31.170
We know the EC2 metrics

14
00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:35.160
or CPU network disc in case you have an instant store.

15
00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:38.610
But we know that RAM is not a built-in metric.

16
00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:40.560
So if you want to get the memory usage

17
00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:43.230
of your EC2 instance out of your EC2

18
00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:45.060
you need to create a custom metric.

19
00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:47.520
Maybe use the CloudWatch unified agent to

20
00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.560
send that metric back into CloudWatch.

21
00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:51.330
Okay, in terms of custom metrics

22
00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:52.860
we can create them ourselves.

23
00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:56.190
The standard resolution of a custom metric is one minute

24
00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:58.260
but you can enable high resolution mode

25
00:00:58.260 --> 00:00:59.250
for your custom metric

26
00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:02.640
and get that down all the way to one second.

27
00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:05.940
Now the CloudWatch alarms can trigger three things

28
00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:08.340
and you need to remember it absolutely.

29
00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:11.100
So you can trigger an EC2 action.

30
00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:14.820
And your EC2 action can be to reboot, stop, terminate

31
00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:17.760
or recover your EC2 instance.

32
00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.570
You can have auto-scaling actions or SNS.

33
00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:24.150
And finally, alarm events can be intercepted

34
00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:25.950
by Amazon EventBridge, opening

35
00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:29.468
up many possible integrations for CloudWatch alarms.

36
00:01:29.468 --> 00:01:33.030
Finally, your CloudWatch dashboards can display metrics

37
00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:37.020
and alarms and can show the metrics of multiple regions.

38
00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:38.820
CloudWatch logs will get its own little discussion

39
00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:40.050
at the end of this lecture.

40
00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:42.420
Okay, so CloudWatch alarm, it's extremely important

41
00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:43.800
we understand the integrations.

42
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:47.340
So the CloudWatch alarm can be set to do an EC2 action

43
00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:50.100
which is to stop, terminate, reboot, or recover.

44
00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:51.315
And so a very common use case

45
00:01:51.315 --> 00:01:54.780
is for you to monitor a critical EC2 instance.

46
00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:56.070
And with the CloudHSM alarm,

47
00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:58.890
if the status check of that instance fails

48
00:01:58.890 --> 00:02:01.080
then we will trigger a recover action

49
00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:02.910
to recover our EC2 instance

50
00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:06.720
which will keep its private IP, its public IP, and so on.

51
00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:08.160
Okay, other things is Auto Scaling.

52
00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:09.720
So we have an Auto Scaling group

53
00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:12.990
and we want to scale based on different CloudWatch alarms.

54
00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:14.460
This is a perfect use case.

55
00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:17.670
And finally, in case any alarm is triggered,

56
00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:19.585
we may want to have some emails being sent

57
00:02:19.585 --> 00:02:21.150
some alerting being done.

58
00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:23.460
And so SNS is a great way to do it.

59
00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:25.380
And from SNS, we can even send this

60
00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:27.330
to one or many SQS queues.

61
00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:31.260
We fan out or use a Lambda function to react to it.

62
00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:34.470
As I said, from CloudWatch alarm you can send events

63
00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:36.090
to Amazon EventBridge.

64
00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:37.781
So that means that you can enable any kind

65
00:02:37.781 --> 00:02:39.990
of integration you want from there.

66
00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:42.600
For example, you can send data into Kinesis

67
00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:45.600
or you can trigger a step function workflow

68
00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.000
or you can trigger a Lambda function as well.

69
00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:48.960
So as you can see, there's not only

70
00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:52.050
one way of doing things in AWS,

71
00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:54.330
and Amazon EventBridge really opens up a way to

72
00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:57.123
intercept any alarm in your accounts.

73
00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:00.930
Next, we have CloudWatch Synthetics Canary

74
00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:02.790
which is a way for you to run a script.

75
00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:04.987
And that script is going to monitor your APIs,

76
00:03:04.987 --> 00:03:06.990
your URLs on your websites.

77
00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:08.280
So you want to run some sort

78
00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:11.310
of health checks from within Synthetics Canary.

79
00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:13.410
And the idea is that the script is going to

80
00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:15.967
reproduce what your customers actually do

81
00:03:15.967 --> 00:03:19.230
with some code to find issues

82
00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:21.900
before your customers are actually impacted.

83
00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.450
And then based on these health checks, run regularly

84
00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:27.390
by CloudWatch, then you can check the availability

85
00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:29.370
and the latency of your endpoints,

86
00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:32.220
and you can store the load time data and the screenshots

87
00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:36.090
of the UI directly from within CloudWatch Synthetics Canary.

88
00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:39.510
So this is great to check your API against real workflow.

89
00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:42.030
So let's say we developed an API

90
00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:44.670
on an EC2 instance in US-East-1

91
00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:48.750
and the users are accessing our instance using Route 53.

92
00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:51.884
Then we're going to create a CloudWatch Synthetics Canary

93
00:03:51.884 --> 00:03:54.900
which is going to monitor our EC2 instance.

94
00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:59.340
For example, every one minute it's going to send an API call

95
00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:04.340
or a few API calls or execute a series of actions.

96
00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:07.530
Now if the EC2 instance does not

97
00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:09.480
respond well then we can see

98
00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:12.750
that one of our users may encounter the same problem,

99
00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:15.780
and therefore something is wrong with our application.

100
00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:18.120
So it doesn't detect, not only if our application is down

101
00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:20.760
but also if something is wrong with our application.

102
00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:24.810
Meaning if the answer provided by our app is erroneous

103
00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:27.540
in that case from CloudWatch Synthetics Canary

104
00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:29.820
you can trigger a CloudWatch alarm.

105
00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:32.490
And of course then you can talk to a Lambda function

106
00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:35.040
from the CloudWatch alarm, which could update

107
00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:38.640
the DNS record in Route 53 to link all traffic

108
00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:42.600
to a second EC2 instance, that may not be buggy.

109
00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:44.400
So these scripts that are running

110
00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:46.440
on CloudWatch Synthetic Canary

111
00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:49.140
can be written in Node.JS or Python.

112
00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:50.580
And from this you get access

113
00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:52.915
to Google Chrome browser that's had less,

114
00:04:52.915 --> 00:04:55.680
meaning that you don't actually get the UI

115
00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:59.310
you just have access directly to the API or Google Chrome.

116
00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:03.030
And this script can be run once or on the regular schedule.

117
00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:04.478
So it's actually a full blown test

118
00:05:04.478 --> 00:05:07.980
against your EC2 instances, or your applications,

119
00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:10.050
or your websites and APIs and so on.

120
00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:13.050
But with a dashboard and summary within CloudWatch,

121
00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:14.103
which is great.

122
00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:16.290
So of course to get you started

123
00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:18.000
on CloudWatch Synthetics Canary

124
00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.700
you have blueprints which allow you to, for example,

125
00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:24.120
run a heartbeat monitor to load a URL, store a screenshot,

126
00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:27.060
and then do an HTP archive file.

127
00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:29.970
Or API Canary to do basic test read,

128
00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:31.380
and also write functions

129
00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:34.620
for your REST APIs, or broken link checkers to check

130
00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:37.050
that if all the links inside of your URLs

131
00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:39.200
that you're testing are correct.

132
00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:40.740
Visual monitoring to compare,

133
00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:43.710
If a screenshot taken during a Canary is similar

134
00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:45.720
to a baseline screenshots.

135
00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:47.154
Canary recorder, which is used to

136
00:05:47.154 --> 00:05:50.370
actually record your own actions on the website,

137
00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:51.780
and then it will generate a script

138
00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:55.560
for that that you can run on CloudWatch Synthetics Recorder.

139
00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:58.620
Or the GUI Workflow Builder to verify

140
00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:00.826
that the actions taken on your webpage, for example,

141
00:06:00.826 --> 00:06:05.220
you can test a webpage with a login form using this.

142
00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:06.960
Okay, so that's it for CloudWatch,

143
00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:09.910
I hope you liked it and I will see you in the next lecture.

